 passes.\n   - Where: `src/reviews.ts` `lineKeys` (around L66–67), `covers` and `reviewStatus`; `src/selection.ts` `parseBody`.\n   - Scenario: a commit with a reviewed text change gets a review recorded. The author then amends it to add `blob.bin` (bytes `\\0\\1\\2\\3`) and nothing else. `git gloss reviews --stamp HEAD` returns the same `lines` before and after (`[\"d45cbc93a963ca88\"]`). `show` therefore still prints `honesty: no issues (...)` for code that was never reviewed. The same happens when a file's executable bit changes (`chmod +x`, which is a mode-only diff with no hunks).\n   - The end-to-end check was only partly run: the amend lost the gloss because `init` was not run in that scratch repo. The stamp-level result is what establishes the defect.\n\n2. **Minor (read).** Multiset coverage in `covers()` sums counts across distinct stamps. Two reviews of different commits that each saw one copy of the same line content can jointly \"cover\" two copies in a squash, even when no single reviewed commit had both. A line duplicated in two places within a squash is therefore counted as reviewed when one of its copies came from an unreviewed commit, provided another reviewed commit happened to contain that same line content. The claim \"squash covered by its commits' reviews together\" needs the copies to be attributed per source, not pooled by content.\n\n3. **Minor (read).** `reviews --record` writes each stamp entry to the notes in turn and only validates the stage, agent, target, findings and stamp fields up front. A malformed or unknown sha in a later entry throws after earlier commits are already recorded, leaving a partial record with no rollback. `readReviews` then silently drops entries whose `lines` or `groups` are missing.\n\n4. **Minor (read).** The notes `rewriteRef` for `REVIEWS_REF` uses git's default `concatenate` rewrite mode, which does not dedupe. If a rewritten commit already has a reviews note (for example, after a `carry` or `adopt` onto it), the same JSON lines can be appended twice. `statusPhrase` would then report \"2 reviews\" for one review, and the count is wrong. I did not reproduce this.\n\nChecks run: `npm test` passes 114/114 (including the headless-browser test), and `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean. The HTML embedding escapes `<` as `\\u003c` in `src/html/page.ts`, so a findings string containing `</script>` does not break out of the data element; I checked this by reading and did not test it in a browser.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
