ample does not match the output (`docs`, verified).** The README shows `reviews: ... bugs: issues found (...)`. The code's `WORDS.bugs.issues` is `'defects found'`, and the scratch repo printed `reviews: bugs: defects found (claude, 2026-10-18)`. The example should say \"defects found\".\n- **Unvalidated `verdict` is accepted and hides earlier issues (`reviews-core`, verified).** The intent says records are \"validated when read, so a malformed one from another machine is skipped\". `readReviews` checks `review`, `lines`, `groups` and the string fields, but not `verdict`, `model` or `mode`. I wrote a record with `verdict: \"maybe\"` and a newer timestamp. The stage then showed `bugs: reviewed (claude, 2030-01-01)`, which hides the earlier \"defects found\" verdict. The stage shows neither issues nor clean, so this breaks the \"reviewed never reads as passed\" promise. Fix: validate `verdict` against `clean|issues|undefined` (and check `model`/`mode` are strings if present), or drop the field and re-derive it from `findings`.\n\n**2. Misgrading necessity**\n\n- **`init-refs` is mostly not required (read).** The `notes.rewriteRef` line for `REVIEWS_REF` serves the task: it is how reviews follow rebases. The glob refspec change does not serve `sync-reviews`. `syncReviews` fetches `REVIEWS_REF` explicitly and never relies on the configured refspec. The glob fixes a pre-existing bug, as the author's own note says (\"found while testing\"). It is a drive-by fix and should be split out or deferred. Side effect: `refs/notes/gloss*` also matches `refs/notes/gloss-wip` (`src/git.ts:13`), so a `git fetch` will now pull origin's wip notes ref if one exists.\n- **`tests` includes an unrelated flake fix (read).** The `carry.test.ts` change (adding `other.js` so the snapshot differs) is a fix for a pre-existing flaky test. It is incidental to the task. It should not be bundled under a \"supporting\" group whose `serves` list covers the review features.\n- **`version` is a release decision, not a dependency of the task (read).** Bumping to 0.2.0 is a release call. The `plugin.json` reformatting (keywords and author split across lines) is cosmetic and incidental. It should be dropped from this change and done at release time. Also, `package-lock.json` still says `0.1.0` (read), so the bump is incomplete even as a release change.\n- **`html-reviews` is arguably `required`, not `supporting`.** The task says reviews should show \"beside the glosses\", and the branch page is the main PR surface. I would relabel it, not drop it.\n\n**3. Removal candidates**\n\n- `version` group: defer to the release. Impact: none on the feature. Only git-gud's dependency pin is affected, and that can wait.\n- `init-refs` glob fix: split out. Impact: `git fetch` keeps failing in repos with no origin glosses until someone pushes one. That is pre-existing, and the review feature does not need it.\n- `tests` flake fix: split out. Impact: none on the feature.\n- `docs` rewording (\"review in a disposable clone\") is incidental. The claim that git-gud \"now runs reviewers directly by default\" cannot be checked from this repo (read).\n\n**4. Simplifications**\n\n- `reviewsLine` (`show.ts`) and the honesty mapping in `run` each call `reviewStatus(sha)`, which re-runs `commitDiff` and `readNote` per commit. `pageReviews` does the same for the HTML path. Compute the status once per commit and reuse it (read; not measured).\n- `groupKey` says serves targets are \"included, without id\". Serves are group ids, so renaming a group still changes the key. Either the note is wrong or the behaviour should be documented (read).\n\n**5. Missing risks**\n\n- **Rebase copying needs `init` (read).** The summary says records are \"copied through rebases\" unconditionally. `notes.rewriteRef` is only set by `git gloss init`, so repos that never ran init lose review records on rebase, the same as glosses. Say so in the README.\n- **Stray temporary refs (read).** `addReviews` builds notes under `refs/notes/gloss-reviews-tmp-*` and deletes it in `finally`. A SIGKILL leaves it behind. It sits under `refs/notes/gloss*`, so the new glob matches it locally. Sync does not push it, but it is clutter.\n- **Unverified CAS claim (read).** The \"test fails without the compare-and-swap\" claim in the evidence was not run. The concurrency test passes with the CAS, so I did not confirm that it fails without it.\n- **Verdict regex (read).** `verdictOf` accepts any of the last three lines that start with `VERDICT: CLEAN|ISSUES`, so a trailing sentence like \"VERDICT: CLEAN is what I'd need to see\" counts. Low risk, but the author's \"must be the last line\" rule is looser than the code.\n- **`--record` trusts the stamp keys (read).** It writes to whatever shas are in the stamp without checking them against the review. The author lists this under self-reported claims, so it is a known risk, not a new one.\n\nVerified: README wording mismatch; `verdict` not validated, letting an invalid latest record hide earlier issues. Read: all other items.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
